    <exercise number="73" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A particle moves on a straight line with velocity function $$v(t) = \sin\omega t\cos^{2}\omega t$$ . Find its position function $$s = f(t)$$ if $$f(0) = 0$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$s = {({1 - \cos^{3}\omega t})} \slash \left( {3\omega} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="74" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Household electricity is supplied in the form of alternating current that varies from $$155$$ V to $$- 155$$ V with a frequency of $$60$$ cycles per second (Hz). The voltage is thus given by the equation</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$E(t) = 155\sin\left( {120\pi t} \right)$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>where $$t$$ is the time in seconds. Voltmeters read the RMS (root-mean-square) voltage, which is the square root of the average value of $$\left\lbrack {E(t)} \right\rbrack^{2}$$ over one cycle.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Calculate the RMS voltage of household current.</item>
          <item>Many electric stoves require an RMS voltage of $$220$$ V. Find the corresponding amplitude $$A$$ needed for the voltage $$E(t) = A\sin\left( {120\pi t} \right)$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <instruction>75, 76, and 77 Prove the formula, where $$m$$ and $$n$$ are positive integers.</instruction>
    <exercise number="75" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$${\int_{- \pi}^{\pi}{\sin}}mx\cos nxdx = 0$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="76" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph math="block">$${\int_{- \pi}^{\pi}{\sin}}mx\sin nxdx = \left\{ \begin{array}{lll} 0 &amp; {if} &amp; {m \neq n} \\ \pi &amp; {if} &amp; {m = n} \end{array} \right.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="77" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph math="block">$${\int_{- \pi}^{\pi}{\cos}}mx\cos nxdx = \left\{ \begin{array}{lll} 0 &amp; {if} &amp; {m \neq n} \\ \pi &amp; {if} &amp; {m = n} \end{array} \right.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="78" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>A finite Fourier series is given by the sum</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\begin{array}{l} {f(x) = {\underset{n=1}{\overset{N}{\sum}}{a_{n}\sin}}nx} \\ {\phantom{f(x)} = a_{1}\sin x + a_{2}\sin 2x + \cdot \cdot \cdot + a_{N}\sin Nx} \end{array}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Use the result of Exercise 76 to show that the $$m$$ th coefficient $$a_{m}$$ is given by the formula</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$a_{m} = \frac{1}{\pi}{\int_{- \pi}^{\pi}{f(x)}}\sin mxdx$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
